# the SPIR-V image writer
#
# a linked module is assembled from the sections the link keeps, each with
# its ids assigned, in the order of the blocks they open and, within a block,
# in input order. its version, addressing model, memory model and byte order
# are the settled traits, and its capabilities and extensions the settled
# facts. a module whose kind keeps no unresolved reference drops the Linkage
# capability, the extension link-once linkage needs and every linkage
# decoration. every module drops the declaration of an import a definition
# satisfies with its decoration and the sections living through it, as the
# definition's id stands for it, and so it does for every input importing
# the name, whether its decoration was kept or folded into another input's.
# of the imports of one name nothing defines, a module keeps the declaration
# of the one whose id the others reach. a definition that lost every name it
# has, as every link-once copy but the one resolution binds does, is dropped
# with the sections living through it, as the id of the definition that beat
# it stands for it. an import's declaration and the definition it binds to,
# and a definition that lost and the one it lost to, declare one type once
# types fold, or the link is refused naming both inputs. the generator is
# zero, as mink is no registered generator, and the bound is one past the
# highest id assigned. an output kind that names its own entry points, as
# a module does, is refused when the module holds none, as no consumer takes
# it. each entry point's interface is completed with the global variables
# its static call tree references through the functions of other inputs, by
# the rule of the module's version. a link takes a linkage decoration, and an
# import's declaration, only as a section of its own, as a module read in has
# them. a relocatable module keeps the sections the link drops, and names
# each by an exclusion decoration of the id it defines, as
# mink.format.spirv.exclude states.

